Looking closely you can barely make out erased markings near the symbols... "<<bunkericon2>><<bunkericon8>><<bunkericon6>><<bunkericon3>>" and words that read "deep" and "landfill".
<br><br>
<<getouticon>><<link [[Put down the dictionary|Public Library]]>><</link>>

:: Read About Horsegirl Racing
Long ago, horses were bred solely for their speed, endurance, and loyalty. As the practice of cross-species lineage became increasingly common throughout the continent, new peoples emerged from those bloodlines. Among them were the horsefolk, commonly known as horsegirls and horseboys. Their strength, athleticism, and competitive spirit soon reshaped the world of racing forever.
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Horsegirl Racing - Modern Era]]>><</link>>

:: Horsegirl Racing - Modern Era
Today, horsegirl racing stands as one of the largest sporting events across the agricultural regions of the realm. Vast racetracks stretch across the farmlands, drawing competitors from noble stables, rural villages, prestigious academies, and independent trainers alike. Victorious racers earn not only wealth but lasting fame for themselves and the families or stables they represent.
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Horsegirl Racing - Tradition]]>><</link>>

:: Horsegirl Racing - Tradition
Although both horsegirls and horseboys share the same equine heritage, organized racing has traditionally remained a women's sport, and not without reason. Historians trace its origins to harvest festivals held by early farming communities, where mares would race for the honor of leading the year's celebrations, a natural showcase for the sustained speed and stamina their bloodlines were already known for. As those local festivals grew into regional competitions, and eventually into professional racing leagues, the custom remained unchanged.
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Horsegirl Racing - Horseboys]]>><</link>>

:: Horsegirl Racing - Horseboys
Horseboys share the same equine heritage, but the bloodlines don't always express themselves the same way. Where horsegirls tend to inherit the sustained sprint speed and stamina that racing demands, horseboys more often carry the raw power and heavier build suited to pulling, hauling, and short bursts of strength.
<br><br>
It's not a hard rule, and plenty of horseboys have proven fast enough to turn heads on a track. But over generations, the sport built itself around the traits horsegirls carried more consistently, and the tradition simply never had a reason to change.
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Horsegirl Racing - Competition]]>><</link>>

:: Horsegirl Racing - Competition
Unlike ordinary footraces, horsegirl races demand a careful balance of speed, stamina, pacing, and tactical awareness. Months of conditioning, specialized diets, and rigorous practice are considered standard before any major competition.
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Horsegirl Racing - Flyer]]>><</link>>

:: Horsegirl Racing - Flyer
Tucked between the last pages, a scrap of paper flutters loose, an old flyer, pressed flat and yellowed, clearly left behind by whoever borrowed the book before you.
<br><br>
<div style="text-align:center; font-style:italic;">
GRAND DERBY THIS WEEKEND!
<br>
Five racers. One track. Only one crown.
<br>
Come watch the Farmlands' finest tear up the dirt for glory, gold, and bragging rights.
<br>
Gates open at dawn, get there early or get stuck in the cheap seats.
</div>
<br><br>
An old flyer advertising one of the Farmland Horse Races. It could be worth checking out sometime.
<br><br>
